---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const cidrHref = toolPathBySlug('cidr-calculator');
const binaryHref = toolPathBySlug('binary-converter');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>How do you convert an IP address to an integer? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Type the dotted address. An ip to decimal conversion, an ipv4 to decimal reading, and an
      ip to hex reading are the same 32 bits. The page returns four writings of that one host: dotted decimal,
      the unsigned 32-bit integer, hex with eight digits, and binary with a dot between each
      octet. 192.168.0.1 is 3232235521, 0xC0A80001, and
      11000000.10101000.00000000.00000001. Any of those four inputs produces the other three.
    </p>
    <p>
      An IPv4 converter here is that translation and the refusals around it. 256 in an octet is
      not wrapped. An IPv6 address is not cut down. A string of digits that is also four octets
      glued together is shown as an integer and, when only one split fits, offered as the dotted
      address. The subnet math lives on the <a href={cidrHref}>CIDR calculator</a>.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the IPv4 Converter →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="integer">
    <h2>How does a dotted address become 32 bits? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Each octet is eight bits. The first octet is shifted 24 bits, the second 16, the third 8,
      and the last octet stays where it is. Those four pieces are added as an unsigned value.
      192 is 0xC0, 168 is 0xA8, 0 is 0x00, 1 is 0x01, so the hex form is 0xC0A80001 and the
      decimal form is 3232235521. The same steps run backwards from a decimal, from 0x hex, or
      from 32 binary digits.
    </p>
    <p>
      Why hex from a packet capture keeps leading zeros is a column-width problem. The hex form
      always has eight digits. For example, input: 10.0.0.1. Output: 0x0A000001, not 0xA000001.
      Packet captures print the wide form, and a shorter string fails a visual compare even when
      the integer matches. Dotted decimal versus a 32-bit integer is only a change of spelling.
      Binary is grouped in eights for the same reason: the octet boundaries are the thing a person checks.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="One host, four spellings">
      <p>
        Dotted decimal, the integer, hex, and binary are the same 32 bits. None of them is a
        network. 192.168.0.1/24 is a prefix, and it belongs in the CIDR calculator, which will
        tell you the network address is 192.168.0.0.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="reject">
    <h2>Why is an octet above 255 rejected? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      An octet is defined as 0 to 255. 256.1.1.1 does not name a host. Some converters accept a
      generic integer in each slot and let 256 spill into the next octet, which silently produces
      a different address from the text on the screen. This page names the number that is outside
      0 to 255 and shows no result. There is nothing useful to offer as a replacement, because
      the typed text was not an address.
    </p>
    <p>
      A decimal integer has its own range: 0 to 4294967295, which is 255.255.255.255. A longer
      number is rejected. A leading zero on a decimal is rejected too, so 0127 is not quietly
      read as octal or as 127. Use a dotted address, or prefix hex with 0x.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="ipv6">
    <h2>What happens when the paste is IPv6? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      2001:db8::1 is IPv6. Cutting characters off the front or the end can leave a string that
      looks a bit like an IPv4 fragment, and that fragment is the wrong host. The page says the
      paste is IPv6 and was not cut down. There is no dotted result and no button.
    </p>
    <p>
      What an IPv4-mapped IPv6 address contains is the four octets after ::ffff:. ::ffff:192.0.2.1 and the
      hex form ::ffff:c000:0201 both carry 192.0.2.1. The page still refuses to pretend the whole
      paste was IPv4. It names the embedded address and offers it as a one-tap fill. After the
      tap, the four spellings are the spellings of 192.0.2.1 only.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Mapped is not truncated">
      <p>
        The button is the whole correction. Until you tap it, the result area stays on the error,
        so a mapped address cannot be copied as if the colon form had been a dotted quad.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="glued">
    <h2>Why does 8888 offer 8.8.8.8? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Digits with no dots are read as one integer. 8888 is the host 0.0.34.184. The same digits
      split only one way into four octets from 0 to 255: 8.8.8.8, which is a different host
      (134744072). People glue octets when they are moving fast. Showing only the integer would
      confirm the wrong host. The page shows 0.0.34.184 and offers 8.8.8.8.
    </p>
    <p>
      19216811 is the case where a guess would be worse. More than one split fits, and 192.168.1.1
      is only one of them. The page shows the integer reading, lists a few splits, and does not
      pick. Add the dots yourself. A dotted address, including 8.8.8.8 after you accept the offer,
      does not show the note. The decimal 3232235521, which is 192.168.0.1 written properly as an
      integer, does not show it either.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Trusting a glued integer">
      <p>
        19216811 is not the integer form of 192.168.1.1. The integer form is 3232235777. If the
        page lists several splits, the digits were ambiguous and the dotted result of the integer
        is probably not the host you meant.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Dropping hex zeros">
      <p>
        Copy the eight digits. A capture that prints 0x0A000001 will not match 0xA000001 in a
        diff. A parser that accepts the short form still fails that visual compare. The wide form is the one this page copies.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>What should you open next? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Matching a firewall log integer to the dotted address an operator expects is the job this
      page finishes. When the question is the network rather than the host, paste the dotted form into the
      CIDR calculator. A /24 of 192.168.0.1 is the network 192.168.0.0, mask 255.255.255.0.
      This page will not invent that mask for you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Binary outside an address, or hex of a string that is not an address, belongs on the
      <a href={binaryHref}>binary converter</a> and the hex encoder. Those tools do not know
      about octets, and they should not. An address that is only a number is finished here.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Convert 192.168.0.1 →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
</style>
